00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:29.949
Watch Later playlist.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:35.350
A fantastic fashion show scene featuring fan
favourites Edna Mode and Jack-Jack was originally

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.710
going to appear in the Incredibles sequel.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.269
In the deleted sequence, Edna hosted a fashion
show of her designs that Jack-Jack hilariously

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:44.160
and repeatedly interrupted.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.280
The clothes in Edna's fashion line, called
"Mode", were worn by models but still drew

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.710
inspiration from the powers of Edna's favourite
clients: Superheroes.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.859
For example, the see-through chiffon around
this model's head was inspired by the look

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:57.219
of a force field.

00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:06.910
Of course, we know only too well from the
first movie about Edna's disdain for supermodels:

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:11.540
"Spoiled, stupid little stick-figures with
poofy lips who think only about themselves"

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.540
"Feh!

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.560
I used to design for gods."

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.290
So, the fashion show was really a way of showing
that Edna was having to work with models because

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.050
Supers, who she really wanted to work with,
were still illegal.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.400
Incredibles 2 is Pixar's longest film to date
and I think this scene was cut because the

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.070
movie was pretty packed already.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.270
Although we do get some fantastic moments
with Edna and Jack-Jack in the final film,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.490
I'm sure many fans would have liked to see
them together even more!

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.440
An earlier version of the story that was eventually
discarded featured Helen donning a disguise

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.561
of a black wig and glasses, and taking on
the fake name Gina Stryker so she could go

00:01:45.561 --> 00:01:47.630
undercover and return to Nomanisan Island.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:51.680
There was a mystery on the island that she
needed to figure out and this could have been

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.240
an interesting alternate story especially
if it had uncovered some as-yet-unknown secrets

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.410
about either Syndrome or Mirage who were both
based on the island in the first movie.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:06.110
The scene where Elastigirl uses her powers
to stop a runaway monorail, while simultaneously

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.750
dealing with masses of traffic and a family
phone call, was initially going to be a lot

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:10.750
less action-packed.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.590
Yes, an early version of that sequence had
Elastigirl bring the train to a halt by simply

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.840
pulling a lever after she'd broken into the
control room.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.000
I'm really glad they explored other options
for that scene as, if they'd gone with the

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.000
initial idea, it would have been a lot less
adrenaline-fueled and we wouldn't have seen

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.330
as much of that awesome Elasticycle chase
sequence, which was a very cool moment of

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.270
Elastigirl displaying her solo superhero powers.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:39.010
The opening sequence with the Underminer went
through many changes during the movie's development.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:43.730
At one time, it was going to feature enormous
clay golems bursting out of the ground, then

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:48.990
that idea was thrown out and they tried robots
instead, and even massive earthworms!

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.930
All those scenarios ended up being scrapped
in favour of the runaway tunneler because

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.100
the idea was that the sequence should work
like a cold open.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.459
It was meant to quickly grab our attention
and get us on board for the rest of the movie.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:05.630
And all the additional golems, worms or robots
could have dragged the scene out too much.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:09.430
Another aspect of Incredibles 2 that was altered
numerous times was its villain because it

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.180
just wasn't working.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.880
That caused some pretty big headaches for
the filmmakers especially when the movie's

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.320
release date was moved from 2019 up to 2018,
which meant they had much less time to try

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.990
out different scenarios to discover which
one worked best.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:28.360
Incredibles 2 writer-director Brad Bird also
had villain issues on the first movie, which

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.890
had a different big bad before Syndrome.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:35.080
However, Incredibles 2 was an even tougher
nut to crack and Bird reckons he discarded

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.240
"two and a half movies worth of scenes" along
the way to creating the final villain plot

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.770
with ScreenSlaver!

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:45.180
Initially, actor Bob Odenkirk's character
Winston Deavor was going to be a shadier,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.599
more untrustworthy figure, along the lines
of Odenkirk's most famous role as criminal

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:53.250
lawyer Saul Goodman in Breaking Bad and its
prequel Better Call Saul.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.310
However, Winston changed significantly during
Brad Bird's rewrites and recording sessions

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.520
with Odenkirk, becoming a more 'innocent',
'positive and upbeat' guy.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.940
Although Incredibles 2 picks up right where
the first film left off, at one point, Brad

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.910
Bird did briefly consider aging up the Parrs
and showing them at completely different stages

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:14.780
in their lives, in line with the 14 years
that have passed since the original movie.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:19.299
But, ultimately, Bird was more keen on following
the example set by The Simpsons which he used

00:04:19.299 --> 00:04:23.479
to work on, as he wanted to further explore
the special insight and perspectives that

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:27.740
each character had on their particular period
of life and he also just wasn't 'interested

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.740
in a college-age Jack-Jack'.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.110
And I'm so glad he made that decision as seeing
an incredibly super-powered baby was one of

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.830
my favourite parts of the movie.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:41.550
Brad Bird came up with the core idea for Incredibles
2, in other words its mum-and-dad role reversal

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.719
and its focus on Jack-Jack's powers, around
the time he finished the original movie.

00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:49.870
However, numerous other ideas for the remaining
storyline were initially considered.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:54.020
The first plot that was pitched, and which
was greenlit by Pixar, involved Artificial

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.020
Intelligence.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.050
The crew started working on this, but Bird
had to abandon it when he realised it just

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.110
didn't work.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:02.029
In fact, Bird has said his scrapped A.I.

00:05:02.029 --> 00:05:05.909
idea could make its way into another movie,
so he hasn't yet given any further details

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:06.909
on it.

00:05:06.909 --> 00:05:10.650
Of course, AI was already featured in the
first movie via the Omnidroid.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.699
Bird then tried another story which featured
the Parrs enjoying the showbiz spotlight.

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:19.509
Speaking to CinemaBlend, the filmmakers explained
that the stakes in this story just weren't

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:24.050
high enough to create good drama and, that
it made the family seem shallow.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:27.909
Eventually at an internal Pixar screening,
it was Finding Nemo director Andrew Stanton

00:05:27.909 --> 00:05:32.270
who suggested that, given Helen's reluctance
to rejoin the world of supers in the first

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:36.810
film, the sequel needed to provide 'a really
strong reason for her to do that' and that

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:41.729
led to the idea that Elastigirl should be
the poster person for legalising supers which,

00:05:41.729 --> 00:05:45.490
combined with the introduction of a brand
new villain the Screenslaver, would properly

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.050
up the stakes for the new movie.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:52.779
By the way, the gorgeous Art of Incredibles
2 hardback book also has some cool concept

00:05:52.779 --> 00:05:57.050
art of outtakes from the movie, including
a story where the Parr family travel to an

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.520
icy, frozen land.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:03.280
And there's an intriguing shot of Bob frozen
under a thick layer of ice, so I'm thinking

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.470
this story might have involved Helen and the
family travelling there to rescue him, though

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.219
that might have felt a bit too similar to
the first movie.

00:06:10.219 --> 00:06:15.009
There are also some very interesting characters
that were deleted from Incredibles 2 including

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:19.569
Frozone's wife Honey and I'll reveal all the
details in my next video.

00:06:19.569 --> 00:06:22.879
I'll link to it here and in the video description
as soon as it's ready.

